  "articleBody": "Repainting a bathroom sounds like a straightforward weekend task, but humid environments are unforgiving. Skipping crucial prep steps leads to peeling sheets of paint, mold growth, and water stains bleeding through your topcoat. Here are common mistakes to steer clear of when updating your bathroom walls.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n- Painting over active mildew: Wiping mildew with a dry cloth and painting over it just feeds the fungal spores under the coating. Mildew must be fully cleaned and neutralized before any primer is rolled on.\n- Ignoring drywall repairs: Tiny cracks around mirrors, towel bars, and trim allow moisture into the wall core. Patching and sanding drywall flush ensures moisture has no easy entry point.\n- Skipping moisture-blocking primer: Using standard wall primer over water rings or bare drywall patches allows stains to leach through and compromises paint adhesion.\n- Applying heavy, thick coats: Piling on thick paint traps solvents beneath the surface. Thin, uniform coats dry properly and build a much tougher, more elastic moisture barrier.\n- Closing the bathroom door right after painting: Without active air movement, damp air lingers and prevents proper curing. Proper ventilation must run until the coating cures.\n\nIf your bathroom walls show water damage or you want a flawless, moisture-resistant finish, professional help saves you time and rework. Contact Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to schedule your interior painting estimate.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
